  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM MDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nThe weak shortwave passing through our area very early this morning \nbrought some light snow showers to parts of the Eastern Highlands. \nMost of these showers will be wrapped up by sunrise and the rest of \ntoday is expected to be dry as we transition from zonal flow to \nsouthwesterly flow aloft. Highs today will be very similar to \nyesterday, generally in the mid 40s to low 50s. Wind gusts today \nwill be around 25 mph across the Arco Desert/INL and around 15 to 20 \nmph elsewhere. The bulk of Thursday will also be dry as temperatures \nwarm just a few degrees into the low to mid 50s in most of the lower \nelevations and winds stay mostly light. \n\nBy very late Thursday night or very early Friday morning, our next \nsystem is just beginning to bring the start of some precipitation to \nEastern Idaho. An area of low pressure off the coast of the Pacific \nNorthwest will be just starting to pull in some Pacific moisture at \nthis time and it's this low that moves inland during the weekend and \nthe overall deep troughing over the West that transports even more \nPacific moisture to our doorstep on Saturday and Sunday. The current \ntrend has been to decrease QPF on Friday, allowing temperatures to \nstay \"warm\" for one more day before more organized showers arrive \nfor the weekend and bring a cool down. On Friday, the more limited \nprecipitation we do have around will likely favor parts of the \nCentral Mountains. The rest of the higher elevations get in on the \nfun on Friday afternoon/evening before showers become more \nwidespread and steady Saturday into Sunday. While total QPF numbers \ncontinue to change from model run to model run, there currently is \nat least a 50-60 percent chance that all of our CWA sees at least a \nquarter inch of precipitation from Saturday morning to Monday \nmorning and in some higher elevations especially the farther east you \ngo, those 48-hour totals may be closer to 0.75-1.00 of liquid \nprecipitation. Temperatures will also drop with highs on Sunday and \nMonday being the low to mid 40s for most.",
